From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SheepyLord <37598021+SheepyLord@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 08:38:13 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Fix a hash race, baker sources and unmarked writes of R13
 memory patches

An independent review of patches 0024-0027 found these:

- The texture hash API raced the image release (0024). dxvk_GetTextureHash
  and GetStableHash read m_image and the released hash without a lock,
  while the device's pass on the render thread moved the image out first
  and wrote the hash after; ToPBR calls the API from Lua's thread. A
  memory-managed texture now keeps its hash in an atomic, written with
  every hash change (SetupForRtxFrom, ClearHash, the release, and kept
  across the image's re-creation), and the API and GetStableHash read only
  that for those textures, never m_image.
- Terrain-bake source textures could stay at their smallest mip (0026).
  "Drawn" was Remix's instance reference count, which the terrain baker
  does not raise: it tracks its sources in the texture cache every frame
  it bakes, and the baked instance binds the baker's own material. Under
  pressure such a preload was released and baked from its smallest mip,
  and nothing restored it. addTexture, which every consumer goes through,
  now stamps the frame, and a texture tracked in this frame or the two
  before counts as in use (textureInUse in rtx_texture_budget.h).
- Writes without a dirty region were lost when the CPU copy went (0024,
  host copies released). A writable lock with D3DLOCK_NO_DIRTY_UPDATE
  records no dirty box, so the write reaches the image only with a later
  AddDirtyRect, however late; the copy could go before it. Such a texture
  now keeps its CPU copy for good (lockKeepsHostCopy in
  d3d9_managed_memory.h).
- The once-a-second pass and its log line ran in every game (0024): with
  neither option on, ManageManagedTextureMemory now returns at once.

Unit checks for the two new rules; the GPU probe's r13 sequence gains an
unmarked write that must survive the copy release, and a thread reading
hashes while images are released.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
